  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday morning through next Thursday)\nIssued at 142 PM EST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nOn Sunday, the low that had been over the Southeast will kick up \ninto Mid-Atlantic, with drier air filtering in across Georgia \nthroughout the day bringing pleasant weather conditions. Lingering \nrain showers in the morning across Northeast GA will slowly move \nout, with skies gradually clearing across the area by late \nafternoon. Temperatures will remain warm, with highs around 70 for \nmost.\n\nModel guidance remains in generally good agreement on Monday, with a \ndecaying frontal boundary pushing across the area out of the TN \nValley. Rain chances look to be 10% or less with this front due to \nthe lack of moisture and it falling apart. Temps will remain warm, \nwith morning lows in the 50s and highs in the low 70s.\n\nBy Monday night, guidance starts to have some considerable spread on \nthe track of a shortwave moving across the Gulf, and another wave \nejecting out of the Rockies. The track, speed, and strength of these \nsystems and how they potentially interact will determine the weather \nconditions in Georgia from Tuesday through the end of next week. At \nthis time, the general consensus of guidance has these systems \nstarting to introduce rain chances again Tuesday, peaking in \ncoverage Wed/Thu, and then a dry/cool airmass moving in Thu/Fri. The \nrange of potential outcomes with the upcoming setup is very large, \nwith some solutions having the shortwaves move through quickly with \nlittle rainfall (0.1-0.25\" or less), while others on the high end \nhave a stronger system developing with a heavy rain threat for our \narea. It is too early to lock on to any of these scenarios, so the \nbottom line is that there is likely to be rain chances in the \narea Tue-Thu next week, with significant uncertainty as to how \nmuch and where. Confidence in the forecast will increase in the \ncoming days, so stay tuned!",
